# Plan part F7b (proposed ADR-0441; `make test-python-serve`), the plan's "done when": `python3 -m http.server`
# serves a file to `curl` in the same room. The server is a background job of the line with a `+listen 8000` word;
# curl is a foreground command whose literal URL gives it the reach rule for 127.0.0.1:8000 (an address keeps its
# own rule). No host server: the exchange is netd's loopback.
! panicked
! first process ended
< Type help to begin, or help tour.
< \r\n~>
# The server. -u: its start line reaches the terminal at once, which is what the next line waits for.
> +listen 8000 python3 -u -m http.server 8000 --bind 127.0.0.1 &\r
< Serving HTTP on 127.0.0.1 port 8000
# The file of the current directory, over HTTP, by a second program.
> curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/served.txt\r
< served by python3 -m http.server over the loopback of netd
< \r\n~>
> curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8000/served.txt\r
< HTTP/1.0 200 OK
< \r\n~>
# A file that is not there: the server's answer.
> curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8000/nothing.txt\r
< HTTP/1.0 404
< \r\n~>
# Nobody listens on 8001: refused, and curl says it could not connect (exit 7).
> curl http://127.0.0.1:8001/\r
< curl: (7)
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 7
< \r\n~>
# Without a +listen word the same server cannot listen: netd refuses (INV-0440-1).
> python3 -m http.server 8002 --bind 127.0.0.1\r
< PermissionError
< \r\n~>
x power off
> poweroff\r
